  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe environment on Sunday looks fairly similar to today's. \nHowever, deep layer shear is forecast to be ever-so-slightly \nhigher (25kt Sunday compared to 15kt today). This may allow a \nslightly improved storm organization potential and perhaps a \nslightly better chance of a few storms reaching severe, or near-\nsevere, levels. The primary risk should be strong winds, but \nperhaps some hail can occur in the tallest, most sustained, \ncores. The focus for convection should be the seabreeze and an \ninland trough. The low-level flow may veer to southwesterly, \nhelping keep the seabreeze less progressive compared to today, \nand storms may not be as progressive. With a high PWAT airmass \nstill in place, this could allow a slight bump up in the risk \nfor urban and poor drainage flooding, with at least a low-end \nrisk of flash flooding.\n\nFinally, a cold front will sweep south through the area on \nMonday. Guidance continues to trend on the slower side with the \npassage, and now this sets up the potential for the front to \nmove through during peak heating on Monday. This will be \nsomething to watch as deep layer shear is forecast to be higher \n(~30kt), and may support a strong/severe thunderstorm risk \nconditional on sufficient instability being realized. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Upper level ridging quickly rebuilds across the\nSouthern U.S. next week. Meanwhile, a notably strong surface \nhigh will nose south along the lee of the Appalachians. \nSynoptically this is a very dry pattern for the region. Despite \nwarming low-level thicknesses beneath the ridge, the \nnortheasterly flow around the high should help to hold temps \ndown some. Additionally, the airmass will be drier with less \nhumidity, making it feel not as hot. It will feel a little more \nlike fall, especially at night and in the morning.",
